$DIR/main.p:14:9: cannot return the function literal: it uses n, a local variable of CountChecked
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it refers to
    those variables where they live, in CountChecked's frame (n is declared at
    main.p:10). That frame is reclaimed when CountChecked returns.
    If CountChecked only checks its arguments and returns the literal, make the
    literal its whole body, so that it is a closure constructor: each call then
    builds the closure in the caller's frame (spec section 4.5). Checks can move
    into the literal, or to the caller.
    Go moves the captured variables and the closure to the heap. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so the record stays in the frame that
    made it.
    Return a value that carries its own state instead, such as a struct with a
    method:
        type state struct {
            n int
        }

        func (s *state) call() {
            ... s.n ...
        }
    (see "Closures do not outlive their frame" in the Plutonium spec, section
    4.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3100" for a longer explanation)

