$DIR/main.p:75:9: cannot return Const(m): Const builds its closure in Built's frame
    Const is a closure constructor: each call is compiled where it is made, and
    the closure it returns is built in the caller's frame, here Built's (spec
    section 4.5). That frame is reclaimed when Built returns.
    If Built's whole body is one return of a call of Const, Built is a closure
    constructor too, and its callers build the closure instead.
    Go moves the captured variables and the closure to the heap. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so the record stays in the frame that
    made it.
    (see "Closures do not outlive their frame" in the Plutonium spec, section
    4.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3100" for a longer explanation)

